  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConvective activity is moving through the lower Ohio Valley\nearly this morning and will continue to lift north today with a\nwarm front and increasing moisture across the region.\nTemperatures will return to the 80s this afternoon with the\nfront and surface dew points will return to the lower 70s for\nmost of the area, giving a warm and humid feel. Storms will move\nthrough the area through the afternoon with this improved\nenvironment in place. There could be a stronger storm possible,\nespecially with some modest MLCAPE across the region and perhaps\n30 kts of wind shear with the mid-level shortwave moving through\nthe region. The main concern would be gusty winds. The slightly\nlarger concern could be heavy rainfall with precipitable water\nvalues of 1.7-1.8\" across the area that indicate that any\nrainfall could be efficient.\n\nFor Sunday, a cold front will approach the region from the\nnorthwest. The main question will be how much has the atmosphere\nrecovered over the forecast area before the front arrives. With\nthe bulk of activity occurring this afternoon, there should be\nabout 18-24 hours for the atmosphere to recover and highs on\nSunday should be back in the 80s with dew points back in the\nlower 70s. Therefore, the front should be able to generate some\nactivity over the forecast area before activity blossoms further\nsoutheast. There could a bit more organization for strong or\neven severe storms on Sunday with a better upper trough digging\ninto the Great Lakes region and better right entrance jet \nenergy overhead. The limitation will just be real estate within\nthe forecast area based on the cold front timing and convective\ninitiation on Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe next weather system will be a cold front that will approach\nthe area Tuesday evening. This front will slow down as it\napproaches the forecast area and eventually stall overhead on\nWednesday. This front will allow for scattered shower and storm\nchances for Tuesday night through Thursday with the nearby\nboundary and the warm and humid air mass across the region.\nToo early to tell if any convective activity will have any\nsevere or flooding threats; however, an east-west oriented\nboundary over the area with zonal west-east flow aloft may favor\nrepeated rounds of activity over the same areas - The question\nwill just be coverage of storms.",
